{"kind":"artifact","text":"Embedded UI bundle at internal/service@3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200, fulfilling planner's request 54c40bf9 under my promise 68f51411. Successor to my candidate artifact 6ff74c6b at b60f3a40, which I am retiring. Companion to the ui artifact 862e8bb5.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. Only the rebuilt embed: internal/service/uidist/index.html, new assets index-BmBDwmX0.js and index-BrSY6LJg.css, and the deletion of the superseded index-Clyj5ZUj.js and index-Dxk4cngq.css. No Go source in internal/service changed.\n\nWHY IT GETS ITS OWN ARTIFACT RATHER THAN RIDING ALONG WITH ui. The embed is committed source that a reader can inspect and a gate can check, and it lives at a different path from what produced it. Someone reading internal/service should be able to see that its shipped bundle moved, and why, without having to know that a ui artifact exists.\n\nTHE CONDITION THIS EXISTS TO SATISFY, and the one I was most likely to fail. Any edit under ui/ leaves this embed stale until `make ui` runs, and neither npm test nor tsc notices — only `make ui-check` does. I have shipped a stale bundle three times on one branch before, which is why planner named it explicitly and why I named it back in my promise.\n\nIt is green here and I checked the specific thing that catches the failure rather than just the exit code: after `make ui-check`, `git status --porcelain -- internal/service/uidist` produces no output. That is the real assertion. A build that succeeds while leaving the working tree dirty at that path means the committed embed does not match the source, and an exit code alone would not have told me.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt, go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module with every package ok, including ./internal/service itself; make ui-check green; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.\n\nA NOTE FOR THE REVIEWER on something outside this artifact's paths but touching this package's behaviour. ui/test/publish-chain.test.mjs, which the modal-focus condition required to exercise the real fold and service chain rather than a stub, spawns a real `gs serve` and drives the shipped App against it. That makes the npm test gate depend on the Go toolchain, which it did not before. On CI both are installed and the build cache is warm before it runs, so it should be near-instant — but I have only run it locally, not on the CI image, and I am not claiming otherwise. If the runner is ever cold, that test's setup carries a build timeout. The alternative direction, a Go test spawning node, would have coupled and slowed `make test`, which is run far more often. Worth a reviewer's judgment rather than my assertion.","body":{"commit":"3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200","path":"internal/service"}}